{"claims":[{"claim_text":"The Republican Party is committed to saving Medicare and Medicaid.", "claim_types":["policy_position"],"evidence_ids":[1]},{"claim_text":"If the programs' fiscal ship is not righted, those who depend on Medicare and Medicaid the most will be hurt first and worst.", "claim_types":["prediction"],"evidence_ids":[1]},{"claim_text":"Medicare will be saved by modernizing it, empowering its participants, and putting it on a secure financial footing.", "claim_types":["policy_position"],"evidence_ids":[1]},{"claim_text":"Saving Medicare will be an enormous undertaking and should be a non-partisan one.", "claim_types":["evaluation"],"evidence_ids":[1]},{"claim_text":"The Republican Party welcomes all who sincerely want to ensure the future for seniors and the poor to the effort of saving Medicare.", "claim_types":["policy_position"],"evidence_ids":[1]},{"claim_text":"The Republican Party is determined to achieve the goal of saving Medicare with a candid and honest presentation of the problem and its solutions to the American people.", "claim_types":["policy_position"],"evidence_ids":[1]},{"claim_text":"Medicare and Medicaid share the same fiscal outlook: their current courses cannot be sustained.", "claim_types":["factual_assertion"],"evidence_ids":[2]},{"claim_text":"Medicare enrollment grew from more than 20 million in 1970 to more than 47 million today, with a projected total of 80 million in 2030.", "claim_types":["factual_assertion"],"evidence_ids":[2]},{"claim_text":"Medicaid enrollment counted almost 30 million enrollees in 1990, has about 54 million now, and would include an additional 11 million under Obamacare.", "claim_types":["factual_assertion"],"evidence_ids":[2]},{"claim_text":"Medicare spent more than $520 billion in 2010 and has close to $37 trillion in unfunded obligations.", "claim_types":["factual_assertion"],"evidence_ids":[2]},{"claim_text":"Total Medicaid spending will more than double by 2019.", "claim_types":["prediction"],"evidence_ids":[2]},{"claim_text":"In many States, Medicaid's mandates and inflexible bureaucracy have become a budgetary black hole, growing faster than most other budget lines and devouring funding for many other essential governmental functions.", "claim_types":["characterization"],"evidence_ids":[2]}],"unresolved_references":[]}
